WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:01.400
- [Narrator] Alarms.

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.912
There's the ones that wake
you up in the morning.

00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:05.636
Telling you've got a message.

00:00:05.636 --> 00:00:06.906
That your lunch is ready.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.253
To close the fridge door.

00:00:08.253 --> 00:00:10.135
Emails, phone calls, Skype calls.

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:13.635
Facebook message, turbulence, amber alert.

00:00:14.756 --> 00:00:17.728
And one particular
researcher has created this.

00:00:17.728 --> 00:00:18.561
(high-pitched beeping)

00:00:18.561 --> 00:00:19.797
An alarm to stand out from the crowd.

00:00:19.797 --> 00:00:20.935
(high pitched beeping)

00:00:20.935 --> 00:00:23.492
An alarm to stop a head on car collision.

00:00:23.492 --> 00:00:24.691
(loud smashing)

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:27.461
Her name is--
- I'm Carryl Baldwin.

00:00:27.461 --> 00:00:28.382
- [Narrator] And she's--

00:00:28.382 --> 00:00:30.371
- [Carryl] I am the Director
of the Human Factors

00:00:30.371 --> 00:00:34.232
and Applied Cognition program
at George Mason University.

00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:35.552
- [Narrator] So how do
you go about creating

00:00:35.552 --> 00:00:37.872
one of the world's most annoying sounds?

00:00:37.872 --> 00:00:40.770
- [Carryl] We started out by going out

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.752
and getting recordings of actual sounds

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:46.152
that were in automobiles currently.

00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:49.872
Then we took those sounds back to the lab.

00:00:49.872 --> 00:00:51.342
- [Narrator] Once they
collected the sounds,

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:53.220
it was time to test on people.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.451
They created a system
where the listener could--

00:00:55.451 --> 00:00:58.609
- [Carryl] Play a sound and
then put it in a category--

00:00:58.609 --> 00:00:59.891
- [Narrator] That the
sound most represented.

00:00:59.891 --> 00:01:00.830
(beeping)

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.811
Either a low urgency,
like a Facebook message.

00:01:03.811 --> 00:01:06.981
A medium range urgency,
like a check engine alert.

00:01:06.981 --> 00:01:09.160
And finally, a high level urgency like

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.080
an oncoming collision warning.
(high pitched beeping)

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.240
- [Carryl] And then from
there we set up a series

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.390
of driving simulator studies
and they're driving along

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.979
and they're doing another
task just to kind of give them

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:24.710
a distraction component and
then suddenly, unexpectedly,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.138
the car that they're following
would brake suddenly

00:01:27.138 --> 00:01:29.590
and we looked at their collision avoidance

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.739
response on the timing of that.

00:01:31.739 --> 00:01:33.299
- [Narrator] They created
the perfect forward

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:35.350
collision warning alarm.
(high pitched beeping)

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.510
And this was really important because--

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.230
- [Carryl] We found that
the sounds being used

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.389
currently weren't perceived
as being very urgent.

00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:45.910
You know, a poorly designed
sound can be more dangerous

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:48.188
than not having a warning at all.

00:01:48.188 --> 00:01:49.746
- [Narrator] So thanks for using the most

00:01:49.746 --> 00:01:52.413
annoying sound to keep us safe.

